    <section class="documentation-section" id="Notes">
        <h2 class="documentation-header">6. Notes</h2>

        <h3 class="documentation-subheader">6.1 General Notes/Lessons Learned</h3>
        <h4>6.1.2 New Situations and Surprises in Part 4</h4>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Database Migrations Gone Wrong</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>I thought running <code>dotnet ef migrations add</code> would be a quick step, but I
                        accidentally left a typo in one of my model properties. The migration failed, and I spent an
                        hour trying to figure out why. Lesson learned: always double-check your models before running
                        migrations, and don't be afraid to delete and recreate migrations if you mess up early on.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Identity Scaffolding Confusion</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>I tried to scaffold Identity for login and registration, but Visual Studio kept throwing errors
                        about missing dependencies. Turns out, I hadn't installed the right NuGet packages. After some
                        trial and error (and a lot of Googling), I got it working. Now I know to always check the docs
                        for required packages before scaffolding anything.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>SQLite Locking Issues</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>While testing with SQLite, I kept getting “database is locked” errors when running multiple
                        requests quickly. I didn't realize SQLite has limitations with concurrent writes. I had to
                        adjust my testing habits and make sure I wasn't running multiple instances at once. It made me
                        appreciate why production apps use SQL Server or PostgreSQL instead.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Seed Data Not Updating</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>I updated my seed data, but the changes didn't show up in the app. I eventually realized that
                        once the database is created, changes to the seed method don't apply unless you drop and
                        recreate the database or use a migration. This tripped me up a few times before I got the hang
                        of it.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Forgotten Navigation Links</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>After adding new pages for order management, I forgot to update the navigation bar. I only
                        noticed when I tried to access the page and couldn't find the link. Now I keep a checklist of
                        all places that need updating whenever I add a new feature.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Session State Surprises</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>I assumed session state would “just work” for anonymous carts, but I missed a configuration step
                        in <code>Program.cs</code>. The cart kept resetting between requests until I realized I needed
                        to call <code>app.UseSession()</code> before <code>app.UseEndpoints()</code>. It's always the
                        little things that trip you up!</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Debugging Email Sending (SendGrid)</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>For password recovery, I used SendGrid to send emails. At first, nothing was arriving in my
                        inbox, and I thought my code was broken. After double-checking my SendGrid API key and sender
                        email, I realized I had forgotten to verify my sender identity in the SendGrid dashboard. Once I
                        did that, emails started coming through. </li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Model Validation Misses</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>I forgot to add validation attributes to some of my ViewModels. Users could submit empty forms,
                        which broke things on the server. After adding <code>[Required]</code> and other attributes, the
                        forms worked as expected. It's a good reminder to always test forms with bad input!</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </section>
